{"winner":"tie","reason":"The two decks share the same structure and nearly the same content, and both meet the core requirements: eight finding titles; Resilient with $440k, $155k/yr, 2.8 yrs and $25k on slides 1 and 8; M4 corrected to 1,080 with totals 5,150/5,670/520 on slide 2; all option cells on slide 3; 124/155/186 with 3.5/2.8/2.4 and −68/25/118 on slide 4; full site table with South flagged on slide 5; Validate AND Train feeding the week-10 gate and rollout in weeks 11–14 on slide 6; R1–R5 at the right coordinates with all mitigations on slide 7; three thresholds and HOLD on slide 8. Requirements lean B. B slide 3 states the tradeoff precisely (+$90k vs Balanced, 2 more lead weeks, lowest 3-yr net, for 99.8% uptime). A slide 3 instead says the 10-week lead means 'the gate moves to week 10', a weak causal claim. B slide 8's 'prove at gate' and 'Must pass at gate (R5)' also avoid implying completion better than A's 'Test before gate'. Legibility leans A. B slide 5 breaks two headers ('Demand/da y', 'Capacity/d ay') against A's one ('Capacity/da y'). B slide 1 orphans '5,670)' and wraps its KPI label, while A slide 1's bullets each fit on one line. Visual communication is mixed. A slide 7 shows scores inside the matrix cells, but its 'Impact (1–5) ↓' arrow is ambiguous. B slide 7 labels impact clearly ('5 high, 1 low'), B slide 6 adds a timeline rule and readable two-line boxes, and A slide 6's 'Design wk 1–2' box is cramped. Design and coherence are essentially identical. These small offsetting differences do not make either deck meaningfully better overall.","dimensions":{"design":"tie","legibility":"A","visual_communication":"tie","coherence":"tie","requirements":"B"}}
```

